          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Introduction to Filmmaking</strong><br>\nAlfred Hitchcock was born in 1899 in London, England. He entered the film industry as a title card designer before moving on to scriptwriting and eventually directing. His early films in Britain garnered attention for their innovative use of camera techniques and visual storytelling.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Move to Hollywood</strong><br>\nIn 1939, Hitchcock moved to Hollywood, where his career flourished. He became known as the \"Master of Suspense,\" creating some of cinema's most thrilling and memorable films. His first American film, \"Rebecca,\" won an Academy Award for Best Picture.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Signature Style and Themes</strong><br>\nHitchcock's films are known for their psychological complexity, suspenseful plots, and cameo appearances by the director himself. He frequently explored themes of fear, guilt, and identity, often through the lens of ordinary people caught in extraordinary circumstances.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
